' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' tar/doc.mfb — the package's compiler-validated documentation.
'
' A compiled `.mfp` ships without source, so an ordinary `'` comment reaches
' nobody outside this directory. Only a DOC block is carried in the package
' binary, checked against the declaration it names, and rendered by `mfb doc`.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DOC
  PACKAGE
  DESC Read and write tar archives -- ustar, PAX and GNU.
  DESC
  DESC An archive is opened either from bytes already in memory or from a file
  DESC you have open, through the same `open` call, and both forms give you the
  DESC same entries with the same contents.
  DESC
  DESC A tar archive has no index, so `open` walks it -- but walking is not
  DESC reading it. Each 512-byte header is read and the data between them is
  DESC stepped over by arithmetic, so listing a 40 GB archive reads about one
  DESC header per entry.
  INFO Tar records a checksum for each header but none for an entry's CONTENTS,
  INFO so unlike a zip this package cannot tell you whether an entry's data was
  INFO corrupted. That is a property of the format, worth knowing before
  INFO trusting an archive.
  SEC `extractTo` treats an archive as untrusted input: it validates every entry
  SEC before writing any byte, so an archive with one unsafe name leaves your
  SEC directory exactly as it was.
END DOC

DOC
  TYPE Archive
  DESC An open archive.
  DESC
  DESC An `Archive` over a file refers to the file you opened without taking it
  DESC over. Closing that file makes later reads through this archive fail with
  DESC `errorCode::ErrResourceClosed`.
  PROP source Where the archive's bytes come from. Internal.
  PROP entries Every entry, in the order the archive lists them.
END DOC

DOC
  TYPE Entry
  DESC One entry in an archive.
  PROP name The entry's name, with `/` separating directories.
  PROP kind One of `KindFile`, `KindDirectory`, `KindSymlink`, `KindHardlink` or `KindOther`.
  PROP isDirectory Whether this entry is a directory.
  PROP size The entry's size in bytes. Always 0 for anything that is not a regular file.
  PROP mode The Unix permission bits.
  PROP modifiedSeconds The modification time, in seconds since the Unix epoch.
  PROP uid The numeric user id recorded for the entry.
  PROP gid The numeric group id recorded for the entry.
  PROP user The user name recorded for the entry, or the empty string.
  PROP group The group name recorded for the entry, or the empty string.
  PROP linkTarget Where a link points, or the empty string for anything else.
  PROP dataOffset Where the entry's data begins in the archive. Used by `read`.
END DOC

DOC
  TYPE Builder
  DESC An archive under construction.
  DESC
  DESC Start one with `create`, add to it, and call `finish`. Each `add` call
  DESC gives you back a new builder; the one you passed in is unchanged.
  PROP pending The entries added so far. Internal.
END DOC

DOC INTERNAL
  TYPE PendingEntry
  DESC One entry a `Builder` is holding until `finish` lays the archive out.
  PROP name The entry's name.
  PROP typeFlag The tar type flag to record.
  PROP mode The Unix permission bits.
  PROP modifiedSeconds The modification time, in seconds since the Unix epoch.
  PROP linkTarget Where a link points, or the empty string.
  PROP payload The entry's contents.
END DOC

DOC INTERNAL
  TYPE MemorySource
  DESC An archive's bytes, already in memory. Use `open` instead.
  PROP bytes The archive's bytes.
END DOC

DOC INTERNAL
  TYPE FileSource
  DESC An archive's bytes, in a file you have open. Use `open` instead.
  PROP handle The file the archive is read from.
END DOC

DOC INTERNAL
  UNION Source
  DESC Where an archive's bytes come from. Use `open` instead.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC open(List OF Byte)
  GROUP Reading
  DESC Open an archive held in memory.
  INFO The argument needs a typed binding: an untyped list literal does not say
  INFO which of the two `open` forms you meant, so it does not compile.
  ARG data The archive's bytes.
  RET The opened archive.
  ERROR 7-705-0004 The bytes are not a well-formed archive.
  ERROR 9316-0001 A header's recorded checksum does not match its bytes.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT tar
    IMPORT fs
    IMPORT io

    SUB main()
      LET raw AS List OF Byte = fs::readBytes("backup.tar")
      LET archive AS tar::Archive = tar::open(raw)
      io::print(toString(len(tar::entries(archive))) & " entries")
    END SUB
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC open(fs.File)
  GROUP Reading
  DESC Open an archive from a file you have open.
  DESC
  DESC The archive is walked, not read: one 512-byte header per entry, with the
  DESC data between them stepped over by arithmetic. Listing a 40 GB archive
  DESC costs about one header per entry.
  INFO The file stays yours. This package never closes it, and it is left at the
  INFO same read position it was found at.
  ARG file The file to read the archive from.
  RET The opened archive.
  ERROR 7-705-0004 The bytes are not a well-formed archive.
  ERROR 9316-0001 A header's recorded checksum does not match its bytes.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT tar
    IMPORT fs
    IMPORT io

    SUB main()
      RES f = fs::openFile("backup.tar")
      LET archive AS tar::Archive = tar::open(f)
      FOR EACH entry IN tar::entries(archive)
        io::print(entry.name)
      NEXT
    END SUB
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC entries
  GROUP Reading
  DESC Every entry, in the order the archive lists them.
  ARG archive The archive to list.
  RET The entries.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC has
  GROUP Reading
  DESC Whether an entry with exactly this name is present.
  ARG archive The archive to search.
  ARG name The name to look for, matched exactly.
  RET TRUE when the name is present.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC find
  GROUP Reading
  DESC The entry with exactly this name.
  DESC
  DESC Tar permits the same name more than once -- that is how an archive
  DESC records a file being replaced. This returns the first, which is the one
  DESC `bsdtar -t` lists first; extracting applies them in order, so the last
  DESC one wins on disk.
  ARG archive The archive to search.
  ARG name The name to look for, matched exactly.
  RET The matching entry.
  ERROR 7-705-0003 No entry has that name.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC read
  GROUP Reading
  DESC Read one entry's bytes.
  INFO Only a regular file has contents to read. A link, a directory or a device
  INFO node raises rather than returning something invented -- a symlink's
  INFO `linkTarget` is where its information is.
  ARG archive The archive to read from.
  ARG entry The entry to read.
  ARG maxBytes The largest size to accept. Defaults to 64 MiB.
  RET The entry's bytes.
  ERROR 7-705-0050 The entry is not a regular file.
  ERROR 7-705-0027 The entry is larger than `maxBytes`.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT tar
    IMPORT fs
    IMPORT io

    SUB main()
      RES f = fs::openFile("backup.tar")
      LET archive AS tar::Archive = tar::open(f)
      LET bytes AS List OF Byte = tar::read(archive, tar::find(archive, "notes.txt"))
      io::print(toString(len(bytes)) & " bytes")
    END SUB
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC readText
  GROUP Reading
  DESC Read one entry and decode it as UTF-8 text.
  ARG archive The archive to read from.
  ARG entry The entry to read.
  ARG maxBytes The largest size to accept. Defaults to 64 MiB.
  RET The entry's text.
  ERROR 7-705-0004 The entry is not valid UTF-8.
  ERROR 7-705-0050 The entry is not a regular file.
  ERROR 7-705-0027 The entry is larger than `maxBytes`.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC create
  GROUP Writing
  DESC Start building a new archive.
  RET An empty builder.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT tar
    IMPORT fs

    SUB main()
      MUT b AS tar::Builder = tar::create()
      b = tar::addText(b, "notes.txt", "hello")
      fs::writeBytes("out.tar", tar::finish(b))
    END SUB
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC addFile
  GROUP Writing
  DESC Add a file entry holding `data`.
  INFO Refusing an unsafe name here, and not only when extracting, means this
  INFO package cannot be used to build the archive its own `extractTo` refuses.
  ARG builder The builder to add to. It is not modified; the new builder is returned.
  ARG name The entry's name, with `/` separating directories.
  ARG data The entry's contents.
  ARG mode The Unix permission bits. Defaults to 420, which is 0o644.
  ARG modifiedSeconds The modification time in seconds since the Unix epoch.
  RET A builder holding the new entry.
  ERROR 7-705-0013 The name is unsafe: empty, absolute, containing `..`, a backslash or a NUL byte.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC addText
  GROUP Writing
  DESC Add a file entry holding the UTF-8 bytes of `text`.
  ARG builder The builder to add to.
  ARG name The entry's name.
  ARG text The entry's contents.
  ARG mode The Unix permission bits. Defaults to 420, which is 0o644.
  ARG modifiedSeconds The modification time in seconds since the Unix epoch.
  RET A builder holding the new entry.
  ERROR 7-705-0013 The name is unsafe.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC addDirectory
  GROUP Writing
  DESC Add a directory entry. A trailing `/` is added when the name lacks one.
  ARG builder The builder to add to.
  ARG name The directory's name.
  ARG mode The Unix permission bits. Defaults to 493, which is 0o755.
  ARG modifiedSeconds The modification time in seconds since the Unix epoch.
  RET A builder holding the new entry.
  ERROR 7-705-0013 The name is unsafe.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC addSymlink
  GROUP Writing
  DESC Add a symbolic-link entry pointing at `target`.
  DESC
  DESC The link is recorded, not followed: nothing about `target` is read, and it
  DESC does not have to exist.
  WARN `extractTo` refuses an archive containing a link, because `fs` cannot
  WARN create one. An archive written with this is still valid and still extracts
  WARN with `bsdtar` or `tar(1)`.
  ARG builder The builder to add to.
  ARG name The link's name.
  ARG target Where the link points.
  ARG modifiedSeconds The modification time in seconds since the Unix epoch.
  RET A builder holding the new entry.
  ERROR 7-705-0013 The name is unsafe, or the target is empty.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC finish
  GROUP Writing
  DESC Finish the archive and return its bytes.
  DESC
  DESC Entries are written as ustar, with a PAX extended header only where the
  DESC fixed fields cannot carry the name, link target or size. Reaching for the
  DESC extension every time would produce archives older tools cannot read.
  DESC
  DESC The result is deterministic: the same calls in the same order always
  DESC produce the same bytes.
  WARN Adding an entry costs more as the archive grows, because each `add` call
  WARN takes the whole builder and returns a new one. A few thousand entries is
  WARN comfortable; tens of thousands takes minutes. Reading has no such limit.
  ARG builder The builder to finish.
  RET The archive's bytes.
END DOC

DOC
  FUNC extractTo
  GROUP Writing
  DESC Extract every entry into `directory`, returning how many files were written.
  DESC
  DESC Directories in the archive are created but not counted.
  SEC Every entry is validated before any byte is written, so an archive with one
  SEC unsafe entry leaves the directory exactly as it was. A name is refused when
  SEC it is empty, absolute, contains `..`, a backslash or a NUL byte, names a
  SEC Windows drive, or resolves outside `directory` once symlinks already on
  SEC disk are followed.
  WARN An archive containing a symbolic link, a hard link or a device node is
  WARN refused entirely, because `fs` cannot create any of them. Skipping them
  WARN would hand back a directory that looks complete and is not.
  INFO File permissions and modification times are not restored, because `fs` has
  INFO no way to set either.
  ARG archive The archive to extract.
  ARG directory An existing directory to extract into.
  ARG maxTotalBytes The largest total size to write. Defaults to 1 GiB.
  RET The number of files written.
  ERROR 7-705-0003 The directory does not exist.
  ERROR 7-705-0013 An entry name is unsafe to write.
  ERROR 7-705-0050 The archive contains a link or a device node.
  ERROR 7-705-0027 The archive's total size is over `maxTotalBytes`.
  EXAMPLE
    IMPORT tar
    IMPORT fs
    IMPORT io

    SUB main()
      RES f = fs::openFile("backup.tar")
      LET archive AS tar::Archive = tar::open(f)
      io::print(toString(tar::extractTo(archive, "out")) & " files")
    END SUB
  END EXAMPLE
END DOC
